    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a residential build out in Oregon. i'm using Douglas Fir for the main support beams. i need to calculate the max span for a 6x12 beam to support a live load of 40psf plus the dead load of the floor system. can u help me figure out the deflection limits i should be looking at so i don't end up with a bouncy floor? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a loft addition in Oregon, you'll want to be mindful of the local building codes, which are the ultimate authority on this, but generally, for live load deflection, you're looking at L/360. This means the total calculated deflection should not exceed the span length divided by 360. It's crucial to factor in the dead load of your floor system—the lumber, subfloor, finishes, and everything else—to get an accurate total load for your beam calculations. You should consult the specific IRC or Oregon amendments for the precise allowable deflection for your application.",
      "source": "target",
