    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that makes sense. i hate it when you think you've got a solid piece but then something weird happens later on. like, are there any \"invisible\" or hidden problems that might show up after the build is done? i'm worried about stuff that isn't obvious just by looking at the grain or the surface of the timber.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Invisible problems, like those that only manifest after the structure is loaded, are often related to moisture content or internal stresses that the wood absorbed during milling. If the Douglas Fir was improperly dried, it can shrink or swell unevenly over time, leading to internal stresses that compromise its strength. Also, hidden fungal decay, especially in areas that might wick moisture from the foundation or framing members, can be completely concealed until it starts to compromise the load path. It's a great reminder that even the best-looking timber needs proper environmental care, just like the whole structure.",
      "source": "target",
